Subject: virtual-net: read a host stream without a read-ahead buffer

A host TCP stream kept a buffer of its own. Asking whether it was
readable reserved ten kilobytes and read the kernel socket into that
buffer to learn a byte count, and a receive then copied out of it. That
is a second place readiness can live, which anything that looks only at
the kernel descriptor - a poll, an epoll set, another thread - then has
to be told about, and it costs a reserve, a copy and a read syscall on
every readiness question. Leaving the bytes in the kernel is simpler.

Ask the kernel instead of emptying it. Readiness is one byte peeked: a
byte says the socket is readable, none says the peer has gone and the
next receive will report the end of the stream, and neither takes
anything out of the kernel. The count the answer carries is then one or
none, which is all any reader of it uses. A receive reads straight into
the caller's buffer.

mio reports readiness transitions and the guest's epoll is level
triggered, so a receive that cannot have drained the socket asserts what
is left, exactly as the listener does after an accept: a read that
filled the buffer it was given may have left more behind, and a peek
takes nothing at all. A read that emptied the socket asserts nothing.

mio's readiness on Windows is one-shot, re-armed only by an operation
that answers WouldBlock, so there every successful receive is followed
by the one-byte peek that decides whether to reassert: bytes still
waiting are reported, and an empty socket re-arms the interest.

